  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m use CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m/[38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m. A shared validator has to pick one, and that choice also decides
  whether the engine can be unit-tested in Node.

  - [1mDual export (CommonJS + browser global)[0m — [38;5;153msrc/validation.js[39m sets [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when it exists and otherwise
    attaches to a global; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads it with a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Buys: works in the browser with
    no build, [3mand[0m [38;5;153mrequire()[39m-able so the engine gets real unit tests. Costs: a small conditional-export preamble, and one
    global name. Matches both worlds as they actually are.
  - [1mNative ES modules[0m — [38;5;153mexport[39m/[38;5;153mimport[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. Cleanest modern syntax, still no
    bundler. Costs: [38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working (ES modules need an HTTP origin, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-click
    breaks), and it's inconsistent with the CommonJS already in [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Testing in Node needs [38;5;153m.mjs[39m or a [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m
    flip that would break [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m.
  - [1mBrowser global only[0m — simplest possible; no export plumbing. Costs: not loadable in Node, so the validation engine
    can't be unit-tested without a shim. Given the engine is pure logic and the single most testable thing here, that's
    a real loss.

  [1mMy recommendation: dual export.[0m It's the only option that keeps the zero-build browser story intact [3mand[0m makes the new
  engine testable, and the cost is about four lines of export preamble.
